1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,510
Have you ever noticed
a woman in a busy café

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:07,160
who seems elegant before she says a word?

3
00:00:07,921 --> 00:00:10,301
She isn't the loudest at her table.

4
00:00:10,621 --> 00:00:12,541
She isn't fussing with her hair.

5
00:00:12,781 --> 00:00:14,881
She isn't scrolling to look busy.

6
00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:18,250
And yet, somehow,
she's the one you remember.

7
00:00:19,005 --> 00:00:22,305
That kind of grace rarely
comes from her outfit.

8
00:00:22,785 --> 00:00:28,145
It comes from the small things she simply
doesn't do when other people are around.

9
00:00:28,979 --> 00:00:34,679
In today's video, we're sharing seven
things classy women never do in public,

10
00:00:35,099 --> 00:00:41,119
and the gentle shifts that help any woman
feel calmer, kinder and more at ease.

11
00:00:41,731 --> 00:00:45,121
A few are tiny.
One or two may surprise you.

12
00:00:45,171 --> 00:00:48,191
And once you've spotted them,
you'll see them everywhere.

13
00:00:48,711 --> 00:00:50,151
So, let's begin.

14
00:00:51,543 --> 00:00:57,063
This one slips out so softly that most
of us never hear ourselves say it.

15
00:00:57,427 --> 00:00:59,847
"Ugh, I look awful in that photo."

16
00:01:00,367 --> 00:01:02,607
"Don't mind me, I'm such a mess today."

17
00:01:03,107 --> 00:01:05,287
"Sorry, I'm hopeless at this."

18
00:01:05,785 --> 00:01:09,545
We say it with a laugh,
often before anyone else can.

19
00:01:10,111 --> 00:01:14,671
But every little put-down is still
a sentence about you, said out loud.

20
00:01:14,831 --> 00:01:17,131
And your own ears are listening.

21
00:01:17,701 --> 00:01:22,561
Elegant women speak about themselves the
way they'd speak about a dear friend.

22
00:01:23,201 --> 00:01:24,981
Honestly, but kindly.

23
00:01:25,781 --> 00:01:30,321
So instead of "I'm so clumsy,"
just smile and say, "Oops."

24
00:01:30,769 --> 00:01:35,209
Instead of "I look awful in this one,"
try "Let's take one more."

25
00:01:35,649 --> 00:01:39,789
And instead of "I'm hopeless at this,"
try "I'm still learning."

26
00:01:40,359 --> 00:01:43,219
A little humour about yourself is lovely.

27
00:01:43,639 --> 00:01:48,431
It's the constant put-downs we're
letting go of. That isn't vanity.

28
00:01:48,971 --> 00:01:51,471
It's self-respect, spoken out loud.

29
00:01:52,833 --> 00:01:58,073
We've all heard far more of a stranger's
phone call than we ever wanted to.

30
00:01:58,482 --> 00:02:03,982
On the train, in the waiting room, in
the pharmacy queue. The phone on speaker.

31
00:02:04,402 --> 00:02:07,862
A video playing out loud,
with no earphones.

32
00:02:08,397 --> 00:02:12,967
But in a shared space, everyone
nearby becomes part of that call,

33
00:02:13,017 --> 00:02:15,437
whether they chose to or not.

34
00:02:15,757 --> 00:02:17,797
And so does your private life.

35
00:02:18,334 --> 00:02:21,394
In public, an elegant call is a small one.

36
00:02:21,854 --> 00:02:23,574
Short, soft, and close.

37
00:02:24,175 --> 00:02:28,815
For anything longer, simply say,
"I'm on the train right now.

38
00:02:28,995 --> 00:02:31,015
Can I call you back in ten minutes?"

39
00:02:31,573 --> 00:02:34,483
Keep your earphones in
your handbag. And let

40
00:02:34,533 --> 00:02:37,513
anything private wait for a private place.

41
00:02:38,011 --> 00:02:40,911
This isn't about hiding your
laugh or your personality.

42
00:02:41,591 --> 00:02:44,711
Be warm. Be expressive. Be you.

43
00:02:45,195 --> 00:02:49,855
It's simply about keeping your call
between you and the person you called.

44
00:02:50,455 --> 00:02:53,915
Discretion is its own
quiet kind of confidence.

45
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:57,470
Curiosity is lovely.

46
00:02:57,850 --> 00:03:00,650
It's one of the warmest
ways to show we care.

47
00:03:01,168 --> 00:03:06,308
But some questions put people on the
spot, especially in front of others.

48
00:03:06,859 --> 00:03:09,919
"How much did that cost?"
"What do you earn?"

49
00:03:10,179 --> 00:03:13,379
"When are you two having a baby?"
"Have you put on weight?"

50
00:03:14,001 --> 00:03:15,881
They're rarely meant to hurt.

51
00:03:16,161 --> 00:03:19,001
But you never know the
story behind the answer.

52
00:03:19,501 --> 00:03:23,781
A money worry, a loss,
a hope that hasn't come true yet.

53
00:03:24,258 --> 00:03:29,698
Elegant women let people decide how
much of their life they share, and when.

54
00:03:30,205 --> 00:03:35,665
So they ask questions that open a door
without pushing it. "How have you been?"

55
00:03:35,965 --> 00:03:38,025
"What's been keeping you busy lately?"

56
00:03:38,649 --> 00:03:43,729
And if someone asks you something
too personal, a warm smile and "Oh,

57
00:03:43,969 --> 00:03:47,329
that's a story for another day"
is more than enough.

58
00:03:47,817 --> 00:03:50,077
Because elegance gives people room.

59
00:03:50,217 --> 00:03:52,637
Room to share, and room not to.

60
00:03:53,596 --> 00:03:57,316
And here's something many
elegant women quietly know.

61
00:03:57,576 --> 00:03:59,676
They've stopped guessing about themselves.

62
00:04:00,503 --> 00:04:03,303
They know the colours
that light up their face,

63
00:04:03,623 --> 00:04:08,703
the shapes that flatter their figure, and
the hairstyles that frame their features.

64
00:04:09,352 --> 00:04:11,532
If you'd love that clarity too,

65
00:04:11,792 --> 00:04:16,772
our elegance and personal style guides
are linked in the description below,

66
00:04:17,292 --> 00:04:21,092
so getting dressed feels
easy, and completely you.

67
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:23,210
Now, back to our list.

68
00:04:24,557 --> 00:04:30,097
This one can happen to the kindest of us,
usually with the people we love most.

69
00:04:30,611 --> 00:04:34,071
You're out to dinner,
and a small remark slips out.

70
00:04:34,531 --> 00:04:36,791
"Well, he never listens anyway."

71
00:04:37,551 --> 00:04:39,871
Or a disagreement with your partner,

72
00:04:39,991 --> 00:04:43,971
your sister or your teenager
starts right there at the table.

73
00:04:44,537 --> 00:04:48,837
And suddenly, everyone else is
very interested in their plate.

74
00:04:49,375 --> 00:04:54,675
The person you love feels exposed, just
when they most need you on their side.

75
00:04:55,066 --> 00:04:58,506
So the graceful thing is
to gently move the moment.

76
00:04:59,066 --> 00:05:04,566
A soft voice, a light touch on the arm,
and "Let's talk about this at home."

77
00:05:05,088 --> 00:05:09,888
Then later, you really do talk,
calmly, just the two of you.

78
00:05:10,168 --> 00:05:12,868
That isn't pretending
everything is perfect.

79
00:05:13,468 --> 00:05:17,048
It's choosing a quieter room
for the real conversation.

80
00:05:17,883 --> 00:05:22,083
Because the people we love should
always feel safe beside us,

81
00:05:22,203 --> 00:05:24,683
especially when others are watching.

82
00:05:25,967 --> 00:05:29,067
This one is so easy to do on a busy day.

83
00:05:29,525 --> 00:05:31,885
The hairbrush at the café table.

84
00:05:32,005 --> 00:05:35,085
A full face of makeup
on the morning train.

85
00:05:35,365 --> 00:05:38,245
A nail filed in the waiting room.

86
00:05:38,673 --> 00:05:41,573
It feels like no one is really looking.

87
00:05:42,113 --> 00:05:44,253
But people are closer than we think.

88
00:05:44,693 --> 00:05:46,513
And while we're fixing ourselves,

89
00:05:46,913 --> 00:05:50,093
we're only half there with
the people we came to see.

90
00:05:50,572 --> 00:05:54,072
Elegant women do their getting
ready before they arrive.

91
00:05:54,692 --> 00:06:00,892
At home, in the car before walking in,
or with a quiet minute in the powder room.

92
00:06:01,341 --> 00:06:04,121
And there's no need to
explain where you're going.

93
00:06:04,681 --> 00:06:08,381
A simple "Excuse me for a moment"
says everything.

94
00:06:09,059 --> 00:06:12,099
A quick touch of lip
balm is perfectly human.

95
00:06:12,639 --> 00:06:16,119
On a rushed morning,
just keep it small and tidy.

96
00:06:16,798 --> 00:06:19,358
Because when the getting
ready happens first,

97
00:06:19,478 --> 00:06:24,938
all that's left is the lovely part:
being there, calm and fully present.

98
00:06:26,333 --> 00:06:30,373
Ladies, here's a small one that
says more than you might expect.

99
00:06:30,979 --> 00:06:35,879
Someone introduces herself as Elizabeth,
and within minutes she's "Liz".

100
00:06:36,419 --> 00:06:41,499
Or someone we met five minutes
ago is already "hun" or "sweetie".

101
00:06:41,918 --> 00:06:43,838
It comes from a warm place.

102
00:06:44,198 --> 00:06:49,598
But a name is personal, and shortening
it is a closeness she hasn't offered yet.

103
00:06:50,084 --> 00:06:54,184
A gracious woman uses a name
exactly as it was given,

104
00:06:54,624 --> 00:06:57,744
and lets the nickname
be offered, not taken.

105
00:06:58,314 --> 00:07:00,654
If you're not sure, simply ask.

106
00:07:01,074 --> 00:07:05,094
"Do you prefer Elizabeth, or Liz?"
It tells her you were listening.

107
00:07:05,627 --> 00:07:07,707
My darlings, where you live,

108
00:07:07,767 --> 00:07:11,787
"love" or "honey"
may be the warmest words there are.

109
00:07:12,247 --> 00:07:15,007
This is only about those
first few meetings.

110
00:07:15,713 --> 00:07:18,393
Because warmth isn't in the nickname.

111
00:07:18,813 --> 00:07:21,853
It's in saying someone's
name as if it matters.

112
00:07:23,178 --> 00:07:27,238
And if you remember only one
of these, let it be this one.

113
00:07:27,778 --> 00:07:32,258
Elegance shows most in the moments
when you think no one is watching.

114
00:07:32,859 --> 00:07:36,079
When you arrive first, or eat on your own,

115
00:07:36,159 --> 00:07:39,159
there's no need to hide behind
your phone to look busy.

116
00:07:39,723 --> 00:07:42,263
Sit back. Let your shoulders soften.

117
00:07:42,843 --> 00:07:45,883
Rest your hands on the
table and enjoy the room.

118
00:07:46,323 --> 00:07:48,143
You're not waiting to be rescued.

119
00:07:48,583 --> 00:07:50,583
You're enjoying your own company.

120
00:07:51,175 --> 00:07:56,455
When someone is with you, let the phone
rest in your bag, so they have all of you.

121
00:07:56,802 --> 00:08:01,032
And when it's time to go, push your
chair back in, gather your cup,

122
00:08:01,082 --> 00:08:04,422
and say "Thank you, that was lovely"
on your way out.

123
00:08:04,883 --> 00:08:06,943
None of it is for an audience.

124
00:08:07,603 --> 00:08:10,983
And that's exactly the kind
of elegance people remember,

125
00:08:11,223 --> 00:08:13,643
even when they can't quite say why.

126
00:08:14,608 --> 00:08:16,908
Thank you so much for watching.

127
00:08:17,088 --> 00:08:20,128
We hope you found something
here to carry into your week.

128
00:08:20,619 --> 00:08:25,799
If you did, a simple like or comment
would truly mean the world to us.

129
00:08:26,159 --> 00:08:31,639
And tell me in the comments which of
these seven you'll try first. I read them.

130
00:08:32,092 --> 00:08:35,202
Be sure to check the description
below for our personal

131
00:08:35,252 --> 00:08:39,612
stylist guide and our favourite
elegant clothing links.

132
00:08:40,130 --> 00:08:42,810
Until next time. Bye, my loves.
